; SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
; Copyright (c) 2026 Martino Pilia
;
; RX state machine: recovers the 10BASE-T Manchester bit stream sliced by the
; comparator front-end back into wire-frame octets. IN base = RXD (data slicer,
; GP8); IN base + 1 = RXC (carrier detect, GP9); JMP pin = RXD. Bits are shifted
; in LSB-first (IEEE 802.3) and autopushed a full 32-bit word (four octets) at a
; time, so the RX DMA moves four times less data than a byte-per-word push and the
; landing buffer is packed dense; the CPU unpacks the four octets per word.
;
; Manchester convention (matching tx_level.pio): data bit 1 = -V then +V, data
; bit 0 = +V then -V. RXD is the sign of the differential, so RXD is low in a
; -V half-bit and high in a +V half-bit. Therefore the data bit equals the
; complement of the *first* half-bit level: first half low -> bit 1, first half
; high -> bit 0. There is always a mid-bit transition, whose direction is set by
; the data bit, so it is used to resync every bit.
;
; Timing (120 MHz, 12 cycles/bit, 6 cycles/half-bit, /1 clock, phase-matched to
; the transmit SMs). Each iteration samples the current bit's first-half level to
; pick the mid-bit edge direction, blocks on that edge to resync, records the bit,
; then advances so the loop is exactly 12 cycles: the SM reaches the next resync
; `wait` two cycles before the edge appears, so the `wait` blocks and re-locks
; every bit (no free-running drift), and the next sample lands at the centre of
; the following bit's first half. Every offset is anchored to the resync edge, so
; the input-synchronizer and comparator delays cancel. The `[4]` advance is
; trimmed on-target against the automated RX self-test.
;
; The SM is gated on RXC so idle-line noise is ignored until a real carrier
; appears; end-of-frame handling (carrier drop) and per-frame realignment are
; driven from the CPU, which restarts the SM during the interframe gap.

.program rx_manchester

.wrap_target
    wait 1 pin 1             ; carrier present (RXC high) -> frame start
decode:
    jmp pin, first_high      ; sample the first-half level of the current bit
    ; first half low -> data bit 1 (-V then +V)
    wait 1 pin 0             ; resync on the rising mid-bit edge
    set y, 1
    jmp emit
first_high:                  ; first half high -> data bit 0 (+V then -V)
    wait 0 pin 0             ; resync on the falling mid-bit edge
    set y, 0
    nop                      ; balance the taken/!taken jmp so both branches align
emit:
    in y, 1 [4]              ; shift the data bit in (autopush at 32); advance to next first half
    jmp decode
